
Here’s a simple DIY guide to make an eco-friendly paper pen — perfect for volunteering events or gifting! 🌱✏️


---

🧰 Materials Needed

Waste paper (old newspapers, magazines, A4 sheets, etc.)

Ballpoint pen refill (without casing)

Glue (Fevicol or any strong adhesive)

Scissors

Scale and pencil

Optional: decorative tape, markers, or stickers for design



---

🪄 Steps to Make Paper Pen

Step 1: Cut the Paper

Take a sheet of waste paper and cut it into a rectangle about 10 cm x 15 cm.

You can experiment with sizes, but ensure it’s long enough to cover the refill.


Step 2: Apply Glue

Apply a thin layer of glue along one edge of the paper (lengthwise).

Avoid too much glue — it can make the paper soggy.


Step 3: Insert the Refill

Place the pen refill diagonally at one corner of the paper (on the glued side).

Make sure the tip of the refill slightly protrudes from the paper edge.


Step 4: Roll the Paper

Start rolling the paper tightly around the refill.

Keep pressing gently to ensure it’s firm and even.

Roll until the refill is completely covered and the pen looks cylindrical.


Step 5: Seal the Edge

Apply glue along the outer edge of the paper and seal the roll.

Press gently for a few seconds to make sure it sticks properly.


Step 6: Dry and Trim

Let the pen dry for 10–15 minutes.

Trim any extra paper at the top or bottom for a neat finish.


Step 7: Decorate (Optional)

Use colorful paper, washi tape, or markers to decorate your pen.

You can also write positive messages or draw small designs.



---

🌍 Extra Tip

You can make a matching paper pen holder using a rolled newspaper tube or an empty cardboard roll — great for a full eco-friendly set!


